(docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #135: `to process` names exactly one process, 1 to
 2147483647. A pid only the running program knows that is 0, negative,
 or wider than kill(2)'s 32-bit int sets the error flag and sends
 nothing. Signal 0 only: kill(0, 0), kill(-1, 0) and kill(pid, 0) all
 succeed when they reach the kernel, so each refused line proves the
 statement stopped before the syscall. The live child is reached by its
 own pid with no error.)

a number called nobody is 1.
Set nobody to 0.
Send signal 0 to process nobody.
On error print "process 0 refused".
Set nobody to -1.
Send signal 0 to process nobody.
On error print "process -1 refused".
Set nobody to 2147483648.
Send signal 0 to process nobody.
On error print "process 2147483648 refused".

a number called wide is 0.
Set pid to fork the process.
If pid is 0 then,
    Wait 30 seconds.
If pid is greater than 0 then,
    Set wide to pid plus 4294967296.
    Send signal 0 to child wide.
    On error print "the child's pid plus 2 to the 32 refused".
    Send signal 0 to child pid.
    On error print "unexpected error on the live child".
    Send signal 9 to process pid.
    Set reaped to reap process pid.
    If reaped is pid then,
        Print "the child is stopped and reaped".
